{visual: add title id:intro_text content:"StatQuest..." duration:persist} StatQuest...
{visual: update id:intro_text content:"StatQuest...\n...is the best!!"} ...is the best!!
{visual: clear}
{visual: add title id:intro_text2 content:"If you don't think so..." duration:persist} If you don't think so...
{visual: update id:intro_text2 content:"If you don't think so...\n...then we have different opinions!!!"} ...then we have different opinions!!!
{visual: clear}
{visual: add title id:main_title content:"StatQuest:\nPrincipal Component Analysis (PCA) concepts in 5 minutes!" duration:persist} Hello, I'm Josh Starmer and welcome to StatQuest. Today we're going to be talking about the main ideas behind principal component analysis, and we're going to cover those concepts in 5 minutes.
If you want more details than you get here, be sure to check out my other PCA video.
{visual: clear}
{visual: add diagram id:cells_diagram content:"15 circular cells arranged in a tight, unorganized cluster." description:"All 15 cells are solid green." duration:persist} Let's say we had some normal cells...
{visual: add text id:psst_text content:"(psst - if you're not a biologist, these could be people, or cars, or cities, etc...)" duration:temporary} (psst - if you're not a biologist, imagine that these could be people, or cars, or cities, etc... they could be anything.)
Even though they look the same, we suspect that there are differences...
{visual: update id:cells_diagram content:"15 circular cells arranged in a tight, unorganized cluster." description:"5 cells on the left side are light blue, and the remaining 10 are green." target:"left cells"} These might be one type of cell...
{visual: add text id:cell_type_text content:"(or one type of person, or car, or city, etc...)" duration:temporary} (or one type of person, or car, or city, etc...)
{visual: update id:cells_diagram content:"15 circular cells arranged in a tight, unorganized cluster." description:"5 cells on the left are light blue, 3 cells on the top-right are red, and the remaining 7 are green." target:"top right cells"} These might be another type of cell...
{visual: update id:cells_diagram content:"15 circular cells arranged in a tight, unorganized cluster." description:"5 cells on the left are light blue, 3 cells on the top-right are red, and the 7 cells on the bottom-right are yellow." target:"bottom right cells"} And lastly, these might be a third type of cell...
{visual: update id:cells_diagram content:"15 circular cells arranged in a tight, unorganized cluster." description:"All 15 cells are back to being solid green."} Unfortunately, we can't observe the differences from the outside...
So we sequence the mRNA in each cell to identify which genes are active. This tells us what the cell is doing.
{visual: add text id:people_measurement_text content:"(If they were people, we could measure their height, blood pressure, reading level etc...)" duration:temporary} (If they were people, we could measure their height, blood pressure, reading level etc...)
{visual: clear}
{visual: add diagram id:data_table content:"A tabular grid with columns: 'Cell1', 'Cell2', 'Cell3', 'Cell4', '...'. Rows are labeled 'Gene1' to 'Gene9'. Each cell contains a decimal number." description:"Data matrix showing gene expression." duration:persist} Okay, here's the data...
{visual: highlight id:data_table target:"all columns"} Each column shows how much each gene is transcribed in each cell.
{visual: highlight id:data_table target:"Cell1 and Cell2 columns"} For now, let's imagine there were only 2 cells.
If we just have 2 cells, {visual: add diagram id:2d_plot content:"A 2D coordinate graph with a horizontal axis 'Cell2' and vertical axis 'Cell1'. 9 blue dots are plotted, forming a downward diagonal trend." description:"Scatter plot of gene values." duration:persist} we can plot the measurements for each gene.
{visual: highlight id:2d_plot target:"the top-leftmost dot on the graph"} This gene (Gene1) is highly transcribed in Cell1 and lowly transcribed in Cell2...
{visual: highlight id:2d_plot target:"the bottom-rightmost dot on the graph"} ...and this gene (Gene9) is lowly transcribed in Cell1 and highly transcribed in Cell2.
{visual: add arrow id:correlation_line content:"A straight diagonal line passing through the blue dots from the top-left to the bottom-right." description:"Trendline showing inverse correlation." duration:persist} In general, Cell1 and Cell2 have an inverse correlation. This means that they are probably two different types of cells since they are using different genes.
{visual: clear}
{visual: add diagram id:data_table content:"A tabular grid showing columns 'Cell1', 'Cell2', and 'Cell3'." description:"Data matrix for 3 cells." duration:persist} Now let's imagine there are 3 cells.
We've already seen how we can plot the first 2 cells to see how closely they are related. {visual: add diagram id:plot_1_2 content:"2D scatter plot with 'Cell2' on x-axis and 'Cell1' on y-axis, showing a downward diagonal trend of dots." description:"Inverse correlation plot." duration:persist}
Now we can also compare Cell1 to Cell3... {visual: add diagram id:plot_1_3 content:"2D scatter plot with 'Cell3' on x-axis and 'Cell1' on y-axis, showing an upward diagonal trend of dots." description:"Positive correlation plot." duration:persist}
Cell1 and Cell3 are positively correlated, suggesting they are doing similar things.
Lastly, we can also compare Cell2 to Cell3... {visual: add diagram id:plot_2_3 content:"2D scatter plot with 'Cell3' on x-axis and 'Cell2' on y-axis, showing a downward diagonal trend of dots." description:"Negative correlation plot." duration:persist}
The negative correlation suggests that Cell2 is doing something different from Cell3...
{visual: clear}
Alternatively, we could try to plot all three cells at once on a 3-dimensional graph. {visual: add diagram id:3d_plot content:"A 3-dimensional scatter plot with three orthogonal axes and floating blue dots." description:"3D spatial plot." duration:persist}
Cell1 could be the vertical axis... {visual: highlight id:3d_plot target:"vertical axis labeled 'Cell1'"}
...Cell2 could be the horizontal axis... {visual: highlight id:3d_plot target:"horizontal axis labeled 'Cell2'"}
...and Cell3 could be "depth". {visual: highlight id:3d_plot target:"depth axis pointing inwards labeled 'Cell3'"}
We could then rotate this graph around to see how the cells are related to each other.
{visual: clear}
{visual: add diagram id:data_table_large content:"A tabular grid with columns 'Cell1', 'Cell2', 'Cell3', 'Cell4', and '...'" description:"Large data matrix." duration:persist} But what do we do when we have 4 or more cells?
{visual: add diagram id:many_plots content:"A 4x4 grid of small 2D scatter plots, making 16 plots in total." description:"A matrix of pairwise scatter plots." duration:temporary} Draw tons and tons of 2 cell plots and try to make sense of them all?
{visual: add diagram id:crazy_plot content:"A single graph origin with 5 or more different axes shooting out in all directions, with scattered dots." description:"An overly complex multidimensional graph." duration:temporary} Or draw some crazy graph that has an axis for each cell and makes our brain explode?
No, both of those options are just plain silly.
{visual: clear}
Instead, we draw a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) plot... {visual: add diagram id:pca_plot content:"A 2D scatter plot with a horizontal axis 'PC1' and a vertical axis 'PC2'. Light blue dots are grouped into three distinct clusters: top-left, bottom-left, and far-right." description:"A PCA plot showing three clusters." duration:persist}
A PCA plot converts the correlations (or lack there of) among all of the cells into a 2-D graph.
Cells that are highly correlated cluster together...
{visual: add diagram id:cluster_circle_1 content:"A hollow red circle drawn around the top-left cluster of dots." description:"Highlights the first cluster." duration:persist} This cluster of cells are highly correlated with each other...
{visual: add diagram id:cluster_circle_2 content:"A hollow red circle drawn around the bottom-left cluster of dots." description:"Highlights the second cluster." duration:persist} ...so are these...
{visual: add diagram id:cluster_circle_3 content:"A hollow red circle drawn around the far-right cluster of dots." description:"Highlights the third cluster." duration:persist} ...and so are these.
To make the clusters easier to see, we can color code them... {visual: update id:pca_plot content:"The dots inside the top-left cluster become light blue, the bottom-left become red, and the far-right become yellow." description:"Color-coded clusters in the PCA plot."}
Once we've identified the clusters in the PCA plot, we can go back to the original cells... {visual: add diagram id:original_cells content:"15 circular cells arranged in a tight, unorganized cluster." description:"All 15 cells are solid green." duration:persist}
...and see that they represent 3 different types of cells doing 3 different things with their genes!!! {visual: update id:original_cells content:"15 circular cells arranged in a tight cluster. 5 cells are light blue, 3 are red, and 7 are yellow." description:"Cells recolored to match the PCA clusters."}
{visual: add text id:bam_text content:"BAM!!!" style:bold duration:temporary} BAM!!!
{visual: clear}
{visual: add diagram id:pca_plot_rank content:"A 2D PCA scatter plot with a horizontal axis 'PC1' and vertical axis 'PC2', containing color-coded clusters of dots." description:"PCA plot for explaining axis importance." duration:persist} Here's one last main idea about how to interpret PCA plots: The axes are ranked in order of importance.
Differences along the first principal component axis (PC1)... {visual: add arrow id:pc1_arrow content:"A long red arrow pointing left and right along the horizontal 'PC1' axis." description:"Emphasizes the horizontal spread." duration:temporary}
...are more important than differences along the second principal component axis (PC2). {visual: add arrow id:pc2_arrow content:"A shorter red arrow pointing up and down along the vertical 'PC2' axis." description:"Emphasizes the vertical spread." duration:temporary}
{visual: clear}
{visual: add diagram id:pca_plot_wide content:"A 2D PCA scatter plot with 'PC1' and 'PC2' axes. There are two clusters on the left that are close together vertically, and a third cluster far away to the right horizontally." description:"A PCA plot with specific cluster spacing." duration:persist} If the plot looked like this...
...where the distance between these two clusters... {visual: add arrow id:vertical_distance content:"A double-ended vertical arrow connecting the top-left cluster and the bottom-left cluster." description:"Measures vertical distance." duration:persist}
...is about the same as the distance between these two clusters... {visual: add arrow id:horizontal_distance content:"A double-ended horizontal arrow connecting the bottom-left cluster and the far-right cluster." description:"Measures horizontal distance." duration:persist}
...then these two clusters are more different from each other... {visual: highlight id:horizontal_distance}
...than these two clusters. {visual: highlight id:vertical_distance}
{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:pca_note content:"Before we go, you should know that PCA is just one way to make sense of this type of data. There are lots of other methods that are variations on this theme of 'dimension reduction'." duration:persist} Before we go, you should know that PCA is just one way to to make sense of this type of data. There are lots of other methods that are variations on this theme of "dimension reduction".
{visual: add diagram id:heatmap content:"A rectangular grid of small colored squares representing a Heatmap." description:"Example of a heatmap." duration:persist} These methods include Heatmaps,
{visual: add diagram id:tsne content:"A scatter plot showing tightly grouped clusters of differently colored dots representing a t-SNE plot." description:"Example of a t-SNE plot." duration:persist} t-SNE Plots
{visual: add diagram id:mds content:"A scatter plot representing a Multi-Dimensional Scaling (MDS) plot." description:"Example of an MDS plot." duration:persist} and Multi-Dimensional Scaling (MDS)
The good news is that I've got StatQuests for all of these...
{visual: clear}
{visual: add text id:dimension_note content:"NOTE: If the concept of 'dimension reduction' is freaking you out, check out the original StatQuest on PCA. I take it nice and slow so it's clearly explained." duration:persist} NOTE: If the concept of "dimension reduction" is freaking you out, check out the original StatQuest on PCA. I take it nice and slow so it's clearly explained.
{visual: clear}
{visual: add title id:end_title content:"The End!!!" duration:persist} The End!!!
Hooray, we've made it to the end of another exciting StatQuest. If you like this StatQuest and want to see more of them, please subscribe. And if you have any ideas for additional StatQuests, well, put them in the comments below. Until next time, Quest on!
